WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
It might be easy to forget that "Xena: Warrior&nbsp;
Princess" was a spinoff. Originally appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.280
as a secondary character in "Hercules: The&nbsp;
Legendary Journeys," Xena launched her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.560
show and blew past "Hercules" in popularity,&nbsp;
eventually becoming a wild ratings success.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.320
"Starring Kevin Sorbo. Michael Hurst.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.840
And, for the first time ever, Lucy&nbsp;
Lawless as Xena, Warrior Princess!

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.400
In fact, "Xena : Warrior Princess" was so popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.200
the spinoff series actually almost spawned&nbsp;
it's own spinoff series called "Amazon High".

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.680
Sadly, though, it never aired. Why?

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.120
The clues can be found in season&nbsp;
5 of "Xena: Warrior Princess,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.240
which featured one very unusual episode. And&nbsp;
that's saying something, considering that "Xena"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.240
Season 5 mostly focused on an evil baby and&nbsp;
Xena's slaughter of all the Olympian gods. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.600
"Lifeblood," Episode 16, stands out because&nbsp;
it includes a time-traveling Selma Blair.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.640
"Lifeblood" recycled footage from the two-hour&nbsp;
unaired pilot of "Amazon High," starring Blair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.760
which would have been a spinoff from "Xena."&nbsp;
Blair played Cyane, a modern girl of 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.760
who by way of a magic dagger is&nbsp;
transported into caveman times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.280
centuries before even the&nbsp;
story of "Xena" takes place.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.000
In "Amazon High," Cyane is considered the&nbsp;
chosen one in a tribe of female warriors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.360
Cyane butts heads with Samsara, played by "Xena"&nbsp;
regular Danielle Cormack, and becomes friends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.400
a girl who can communicate with animals. She's&nbsp;
then captured by a tribe of cannibals led by Kor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.760
played by another "Xena" regular, future film&nbsp;
star Karl Urban. Cyane later befriends Kor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.160
domesticates horses, and names her&nbsp;
new tribe of warrior women "Amazons."

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.480
So why didn't "Amazon High" work out?&nbsp;
The show's main writer, Rob Tapert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.680
had a pretty impressive hit rate. Tapert created&nbsp;
"Xena," then moved on to other syndicated shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:52.160
like "Cleopatra 2525" and "Young Hercules."&nbsp;
"Amazon High" seemed like a sure thing, right?

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.120
Well, it should have been. Syndicated shows&nbsp;
were big business in the early '90s. "Baywatch"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.680
proved that such shows could be financially&nbsp;
profitable. And according to the LA Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.640
"Xena" and its predecessor "Hercules" actually&nbsp;
outperformed "Baywatch" at one point. It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.480
make sense to try and get another show&nbsp;
out of the swords and sandals franchise.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.840
The problem for Tapert was that the&nbsp;
success of "Xena" and "Hercules"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.720
spawned a lot of imitators. Tapert told the&nbsp;
website "Book of the Dead" that he decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.960
axe "Amazon High" at the very moment they&nbsp;
signed a deal to make the show, saying,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.040
"Everybody was pleased with what they bought.&nbsp;
And I was standing at a monitor [...] watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.880
'The Lost World' promo, and I said, 'You&nbsp;
know what, we've been ripped off so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.640
times [...] we have to do something fresh.'"
Around this time, there were similar shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.080
on the air about Tarzan, Sinbad, and Conan&nbsp;
the Barbarian. Tapert told the "LA Times",

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.080
"The 'Baywatch' people are doing Sinbad, and&nbsp;
of course there are a bunch of other rip-offs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.480
on the way. I mean, what are we going&nbsp;
to do: 'Young Moses, Freedom Fighter'?"

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.280
So Tapert took the basic "'90s woman time&nbsp;
travels back to a more savage time" plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.560
and flipped it, creating "Cleopatra&nbsp;
2525" - a show about a modern exotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.240
dancer traveling forward in time and&nbsp;
fighting the robots that have taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.720
over the earth. It was an early breakout&nbsp;
role for "Firefly" star Gina Torres.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.920
While we'll never know how things would&nbsp;
have turned out for "Amazon High,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.520
Tapert may have had the right idea about needless&nbsp;
imitators flooding the market with copycat shows.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:16.960
But at least we got "Xena!"

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.888
"From this moment on, we are the amazons!"

00:03:22.888 --> 00:03:25.840
"Amazons rule!!!"

